 These", -1.7757220268249512]]}, {"t": " CCS", "lp": -0.7064871788024902, "top": [[" CCS", -0.7064871788024902], [" `", -0.9760604500770569]]}, {"t": " mode", "lp": -0.5715538263320923, "top": [[" mode", -0.5715538263320923], [" numbering", -2.1410367488861084]]}, {"t": " options", "lp": -1.7666925191879272, "top": [[" options", -1.7666925191879272], [" parameter", -2.00105619430542]]}, {"t": " appear", "lp": -0.46225669980049133, "top": [[" appear", -0.46225669980049133], [" are", -2.2102787494659424]]}, {"t": " in", "lp": -0.03485832363367081, "top": [[" in", -0.03485832363367081], [" primarily", -4.325297832489014]]}, {"t": " tools", "lp": -0.17485742270946503, "top": [[" tools", -0.17485742270946503], [" some", -2.585049867630005]]}, {"t": " like", "lp": -0.028577443212270737, "top": [[" like", -0.028577443212270737], [" and", -4.493281841278076]]}, {"t": " `", "lp": -0.8382968306541443, "top": [[" `", -0.8382968306541443], [" **", -1.9171913862228394]]}, {"t": "intel", "lp": -1.4508451223373413, "top": [["intel", -1.4508451223373413], ["arc", -1.7277101278305054]]}, {"t": "_gpu", "lp": -0.12330798804759979, "top": [["_gpu", -0.12330798804759979], ["-com", -3.3868348598480225]]}, {"t": "_top", "lp": -0.025515874847769737, "top": [["_top", -0.025515874847769737], ["_tools", -3.8980019092559814]]}, {"t": "`,", "lp": -0.36802181601524353, "top": [["`,", -0.36802181601524353], ["`", -1.178425669670105]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nCCS stands for **Compute Control Station** (or \"Compute Control Slice\"). These are dedicated compute cores on the GPU that handle scheduling and management tasks, separate from the main compute cores that do the heavy lifting.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**CCS Mode 1:**\n- Uses **CCS0** (the first compute control station)\n- This is the default and most common configuration\n- The primary CCS handles the workload dispatch\n\n**CCS Mode 2:**\n- Uses **CCS1** (the second compute control station)\n- An alternative CCS for handling compute operations\n\n**CCS Mode 4:**\n- Uses **CCS2** (the third compute control station)\n\n## When to Use Each\n\nThe practical answer is that **for most users, you don't need to change these at all** \u2014 Mode 1 is the sensible default.\n\nThe modes matter primarily in these scenarios:\n\n- **Debugging / profiling:** Different CCS modes can help isolate issues or understand how the driver distributes work across the GPU's control stations.\n- **Specific compute workloads:** In rare cases, certain workloads or driver versions may behave differently depending on which CCS is used.\n- **Testing hardware/driver behavior:** Developers may experiment with different modes to understand scheduling.\n\n## Practical Advice\n\nUnless you're doing low-level driver development, profiling, or troubleshooting a specific issue, **stick with Mode 1**. There's generally no performance benefit to switching modes for typical gaming or compute workloads, and changing them without understanding the underlying hardware behavior could lead to unexpected issues.\n\n---\n\n**A note of honesty:** My knowledge of the exact semantics of these specific mode numbers in the `xe` driver may not be fully current or precise. The CCS mode options appear in tools like `intel_gpu_top`,"}